thin-slice "TS.260821.01" {
    slug "declare-an-entity-kind"
    title "Refuse a record whose kind nothing declares, and say what to declare"
    kind "command"
    realizes "CAP.delivery-record"
    from-storm "ES.260819.01"
    state "proposed"

    command "declare-entity"
    actor "maintainer"
    produces-event "EntityDeclared" "EntityDeclarationRefused"
    needs-read-model "capabilities-and-what-they-own"

    trigger "the model gained a kind of thing the record does not yet know about"
    outcome "the kind is declared and its records are checked, or a record using an undeclared kind is refused by name"

    scenario #"""
        Given a record file holding a node whose kind the schema does not declare
        When the record is checked
        Then it is refused, naming the kind and saying that declaring it is an amendment to the
             architecture rather than an edit to the engine — because three entities went missing
             this way already, and each was found only when something tried to build against it.
        """#

    step "1" does="write the kind on the architecture's schema block, with what it IS and the fields it must carry"
    step "2" does="records of that kind are checked from then on, with no engine change"
    step "3a" does="on a record whose kind is undeclared, refuse and name it" when="undeclared"
    step "3b" does="on a kind declared with no fields, admit and report — a shape that asks nothing cannot refuse anything" when="declared but empty"

    excludes "deciding whether the kind SHOULD exist. That is the architecture's argument, not the checker's"
    excludes "inventing a shape for an undeclared kind. The engine may not encode what the record declares (ADR.260819.01/A4)"
    excludes "refusing an unknown PROPERTY on a known kind. Fields are declared; properties are not, and widening that is a separate cut"

    layer "doctrine"    reaches="the skill teaches declaring the kind before writing records of it, and that extending the vocabulary is an amendment rather than a code change"
    layer "enforcement" reaches="undeclared-entity-kind, which the schema has declared since NA.260820.01 and nothing has ever enforced"
    layer "docs"        reaches="the refusal names the kind and points at where declaring it belongs"

    claim "C1" text="a node whose kind the schema does not declare is refused, naming the kind" \
        settled-by="a negative test with an undeclared kind, asserting the kind appears in the diagnostic"
    claim "C2" text="declaring the kind on the schema admits it, with no engine change" \
        settled-by="a test that adds a kind to a schema fixture and asserts the same record then passes"
    claim "C3" text="a kind declared with no fields is admitted and reported, never silently accepted" \
        settled-by="a test asserting a report rather than a refusal, and asserting it is not silent"
    claim "C4" text="the three kinds that went undeclared — iteration, read-model, phase — would each have been caught" \
        settled-by="a regression test replaying each against the schema as it stood before that kind was declared"

    depends-on "the schema's entity-and-field shape, frozen — the same contract TS.260820.01 built against"

    useful-alone "the record stops being able to grow a kind of thing in silence. Nothing else in \
        this frame needs to ship for that to be worth having"

    attacks "S2" "S7"

    cut-from "ITER.260820.01/P9" \
        because="the rule was declared on the architecture and never enforced, so exactly the \
            entities that were missing are the ones the checker could not have flagged. Three of \
            the first iteration's nine findings are instances of this one gap"
    note "cut with the checker the previous iteration built. It is the first slice whose shape was \
        refused-or-admitted rather than asserted by its author"
}
